  "title": "DAR Rejected a Bid to Bar Transgender Women. Why Did It Disband the Texas Chapter That Led the Push?",
  "deck": "An East Texas chapter says it was punished for pressing the Daughters of the American Revolution to change its membership rules. National leaders say the chapter used its official resources to advance an outside campaign. A Texas judge temporarily restored the chapter’s standing while the dispute moves through court.",
  "body": [
    "In June, delegates to the Daughters of the American Revolution voted on whether to limit membership to women who were female at birth. The proposal would have barred transgender women whose government records identify them as female. Delegates rejected it, 1,474 to 981, according to the national organization.",
    "Four months later, DAR’s national board voted to disband the Martha Laird Chapter in Mount Pleasant, Texas, which had helped lead the push for the restriction. The chapter sued on November 2, arguing that national leaders punished it for using the organization’s own procedures to challenge a membership policy. DAR says the chapter crossed a different line when it used its name, leadership positions and communications to advance an outside advocacy group’s campaign.",
    "Both sides say members are entitled to seek changes to DAR’s rules. Their dispute is over what a chapter can do collectively and whether Martha Laird was given a fair chance to defend its actions before the board voted.",
    "The vote left the membership rules in place",
    "DAR’s public membership guidance says women 18 and older can apply if they can document direct descent from a person who aided American independence. In an October message to members, DAR’s president general said the bylaws have no sex-at-birth requirement and the organization has long relied on government-issued documentation. The June proposal sought to change how DAR interprets the word woman in those bylaws. Its defeat left the existing rules in place.",
    "The Texas chapter also disputes where and how the proposal was heard. Its petition says the bylaws require a special meeting upon written requests from at least 30 chapters in 10 states. The chapter says it had support from 68 chapters in 27 states and that leadership put the issue before the annual convention after the deadline for credentialing voting delegates. DAR’s president general says delegates received advance notice and debated the proposal for nearly three hours before voting by secret ballot. Those accounts leave a procedural question open: Did the convention vote satisfy the bylaws once enough chapters had asked for a separate meeting?",
    "What did the chapter do, and when was it told?",
    "National DAR says the outside group had its own leadership, funding and organizing network. It says Martha Laird used its official authority and resources to further that group’s campaign and failed to provide records the national organization requested. The board cited a bylaw covering conduct that discredits the national society. Individual members could retain their DAR memberships and continue advocating for changes.",
    "The chapter says its resolutions and outreach were permitted under DAR’s governing rules. Its petition alleges that national leaders gave their specific explanation for disbandment only after the vote and never gave the chapter an opportunity to respond to those allegations beforehand. The board’s requests, the chapter’s replies and any notice sent before October 10 would show what the chapter knew it was being asked to defend.",
    "The Texas case puts the chapter’s future and property at stake",
    "The Martha Laird Chapter is separately incorporated as a Texas nonprofit. It says that after the disbandment vote, it was directed to wind up its affairs and turn over its funds and other property. The chapter argues that the national board’s authority over DAR affiliation gives neither the national nor state society the power to dissolve a Texas corporation or take control of its assets. It is asking the court to set aside the disbandment and protect its corporate existence and property.",
    "A Titus County judge temporarily restored the chapter’s recognized status and restricted interference with its funds while the case proceeds. PJ Media reported that the injunction hearing was moved to early 2027. The exact date could not be verified from the court docket.",
    "The membership vote settled the proposal before delegates in June. The lawsuit now asks how far national leaders can go when a local chapter keeps pressing for change, and what process that chapter is owed before it loses its place in the organization."
  ],
